    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s11">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Peripheral and Central Structures of the Auditory System. Panel A shows the outer, middle, and inner ear. Panel B depicts a cross-section of the cochlea. Panel C illustrates the ascending auditory pathway. Panel D demonstrates the ascending pathways (red/black), descending pathways from the cortex (orange) and brainstem (blue), and crossed auditory pathways (blue). Panel E presents a model representation of an acoustic neuroma. All imaging was created by Diana Peterson for the current manuscript.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Auditory__Circuits" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s12">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Right Auditory Ossicles. This image shows the anterior aspect of the right chain of ossicles (malleus, incus, and stapes) and their ligaments. Other structures in this illustration include the tympanic membrane, tympanic cavity, and vestibule. Henry Vandyke Carter, Public Domain, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray919" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s13">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Inner Ear Anatomy. This illustration shows the semicircular ducts and parts of the cochlea. Blausen.com staff. Medical Gallery of Blausen Medical 2014. <italic toggle="yes">WikiJournal of Medicine.</italic> doi: 10.15347/wjm/2014.010.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Blausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s14">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Diagrammatic Longitudinal Section of the Cochlea.&#x000a0;This illustration shows the scala vestibuli, scala media, scala tympani&#x000a0;and cochlear nerve. Henry Vandyke Carter, Public Domain, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray928" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s15">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Organ of Corti. Cross-section of the cochlea (A). Section through the organ of Corti (B). (A)&#x000a0;Contributed by D Peterson, MD(B) Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;search=Organ+of+Corti+gray+plate&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Figure__1A" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s16">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Auditory Pathways. A. This illustration represents the ascending auditory circuits. B. In this combined illustration, the ascending auditory pathway is&#x000a0;represented by the red and black arrows, which stand for binaural ascending signals.&#x000a0;The descending pathway is represented by the orange arrows&#x000a0;(for cortical impulses) and&#x000a0;blue&#x000a0;arrows&#x000a0;(for brainstem signals). Crossing arrows&#x000a0;represent&#x000a0;the interconnections between the various auditory centers. Contributed and created by Diana Peterson.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Figure__3ab" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s17">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Lymph Nodes of the Head and Neck. The illustration shows major lymph node groups of the head and neck, labeled in anatomical context. Nodes are indicated as green ovals, with regional names identifying their location. Contributed by Chelsea Rowe</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="supraclavicular__lymph__node" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18007.s18">
        <fig id="article-18007.image.f8" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Muscles Around the Pharyngotympanic Tube. This illustration shows the tensor veli palatini and levator veli palatini, which assist in opening the&#x000a0;pharyngotympanic tube, also known as the eustachian or auditory tube. Illustration by E Gregory</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="0-1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
